{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0b9b19a924b846190169bb296631248191a58ab67cd012d9b1", "created": 1773873510, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Medical Imaging Contrast Use: Contrast Material, Allergic Reaction Risk, and Pelvic MRI Follow-up\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on the clinical use of contrast material in medical imaging and the safety considerations that govern when and how contrast is administered. The core entity, CONTRAST MATERIAL, is connected to (1) its elimination route through urine, (2) contraindication or caution in the setting of serious allergy and kidney dysfunction, and (3) allergy-related reporting considerations. A closely related subcluster addresses ALLERGIC REACTION TO CONTRAST and the mitigation pathway via medicines (including allergy premedication). A smaller procedural pathway connects INTRAVENOUS (IV) CONTRAST to administration through a vein, while an imaging follow-up pathway links MRI OF THE PELVIS WITH CONTRAST to fertility-sparing treatment and a defined surveillance schedule.\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"Contrast material is a central clinical tool with modality-dependent use and typical renal elimination\",\"explanation\":\"CONTRAST MATERIAL is described as a non-permanent substance used to improve visibility of internal body structures on imaging, and it is commonly referenced in exams such as “CT with contrast” and “pelvic MRI with contrast,” with the specific contrast type depending on modality (CT vs MRI) and the administration route varying (oral vs intravenous). These points establish contrast as the hub of the community and explain why multiple safety and process relationships attach to it. [Data: Entities (1522)]\\n\\nAfter imaging, contrast is described as leaving the body, most often via urine, and a specific relationship explicitly states urine as the elimination 